Title: Clarify worktree-paths rule — main-branch exception for housekeeping
Created: 2026-05-20
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-05-20T22:30Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

The `rules/workflow.md` "Worktree paths" section currently says:

> In an `EnterWorktree` session, `Edit`/`Write`/`Read` tools accept any absolute
> path. Edits at `~/<repo>/<file>` go to the **main repo**, not the
> worktree. Always use worktree-rooted paths. Confirm with `pwd` and
> `git branch --show-current` before committing — if branch is `main`, stop.

The strict "if branch is main, stop" advice collides with `rules/git.md` which
explicitly allows main commits for housekeeping:

> Always work on a branch. Main is read-only except for STATE housekeeping.

In practice, ticket lifecycle commits (close ticket, open ticket, batch close
several) routinely land directly on main with a `tickets: ...` prefix — and
this is the documented project norm in at least one project (AEDIST), with a
long commit history of precedent (a81a452, 85a0e6c, 61b00d2, 8619f94).

An agent following workflow.md literally stops at a legitimate housekeeping
commit. An agent ignoring workflow.md drifts toward making real code changes
on main. Both fail modes are real.

## Actions

1. Update `rules/workflow.md` "Worktree paths" section to enumerate the
   accepted main-commit categories instead of a blanket "stop":
   - STATE.md updates (already covered by git.md)
   - Ticket lifecycle: closing finished tickets (adding `Closed:` header +
     log entry), opening new tickets (creating `.erg` files), batch ticket
     housekeeping
   - Memory / configuration files outside the worktree's purview
   For everything else (code, manuscript prose, data files), the "stop"
   guidance still applies.

2. Cross-reference: link this clarification to `git.md`'s main-readonly rule
   so the two rules read as one coherent policy rather than two
   contradictory ones.

3. Consider: should there be a positive list of `tickets:` and `STATE:`
   commit message prefixes that the verify-adherence skill recognizes as
   sanctioned main commits? Out of scope for this ticket but worth a
   follow-up.

## Test

There is no automated test for prose rules. Validation is by reading:
- The updated section in `rules/workflow.md` enumerates the categories
- A reader who picks up only `workflow.md` does not get a rule that
  contradicts `git.md`

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `rules/workflow.md` "Worktree paths" updated with the enumerated
      exception list
- [ ] Cross-reference to `git.md` "Main is read-only except for STATE
      housekeeping" added
- [ ] No remaining `if branch is main, stop` unconditional in workflow.md

## Notes

- This is rules-only, no code touched.
- Triggered by AEDIST session 2026-05-20: agent wrote ticket files to
  `~/<repo>/tickets/...` from within a worktree, committed them
  on main, and pushed without incident — which workflow.md's literal reading
  would have prevented but git.md's reading explicitly allows.
